<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12453"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mel40<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mel40<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 8:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tac,  <BR> <BR>I agree with you they are life long predators that are unable to be &#34;corrected&#34;. <BR> <BR>I am doing research with someone who has been working with these child predators and why 15 years.  And I asked can they can change and I quote <BR> &#34;They are people with serious problems and <BR>in my own way, I guess I rationalize it as my way of preventing further victimization <BR>&#40;if possible&#41;.  It&#39;s really up to the person to manage their behavior.  Sex <BR>offending cannot be &#34;cured&#34;.  Preventing child molestation and abduction, I <BR>think it is possible to some extent.  People don&#39;t want to talk about the subjects <BR>and it just helps offenders.&#34;   <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
