<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12467"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 2:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Just a thought here...If you can find where where Bindner worked as a grave digger, and the time that he worked there, then compare that to the names of the people buried at that time, you would have a list of graves that may have children buried in &#34;sub-graves&#34;. <BR> <BR>Since they were presumeably buried without coffins it is safe to assume that they have decomposed to some extent, but being 6&#43; feet deep, there are probably still skeletal remains.  <BR> <BR>The decay would leave a cavity in the earth, beneath the casket, which could be easily detected using sonic equipment like that used in mining, road construction, finding buried pipe, oil fields etc.. The remains could be retrieved by digging adjacent to the grave and removing them laterally, without having to exhume the casket on top.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
