<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12490"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tallulabay<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tallulabay<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 1:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Logistically as my veteran father became a gravedigger in 1957, the graves could not hold a body pre coffin, for one the decay, two the insect population at that time, and three the amount of soil displacement, and most of all four, access. Therefore if anyone has said they are buried below as a result of the dna found on a certain grave it is a red herring. The times and dates were for  a reason, as premeditated. The fact that a skull was found nearly a year later is someone has either medical knowledge or knowledge of graves. The easiest way is to copy a known killers MO from newspaper ads and that fits, so the rest is imprinted. This is now with the proper authorities and stand by this. We are out there looking as it is spring. Who are they copying, it is obvious.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
