<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12647"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:kathiemoffett@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Dahlia<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dahlia<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 10:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Just to clarify re: Curtis Dean Anderson~ <BR> <BR>This article was dated June of last year. <BR> <BR>&#34;Court Rejects Appeal by Curtis Dean Anderson <BR>A Vallejo man convicted of kidnapping and molesting an eight-year-old girl lost what may be his last bid to avoid spending the rest of his life behind bars. <BR> <BR>A state appellate court rejected an appeal by Curtis Dean Anderson, who is currently serving a 251-year sentence. In the appeal, Anderson contended that his conviction should be overturned because his attorney had failed to renew a change of venue motion, had improperly submitted an unedited transcript to the jury, and had not objected to testimony by an expert on sexual assaults. <BR> <BR>The three-judge panel ruled that the lawyer&#39;s actions were consistent with those of a &#34;reasonably competent attorney,&#34; and found no reason why the conviction should be vacated. <BR> <BR>The 43-year-old Anderson is facing murder, kidnapping and molestation charges in the case of seven-year-old Xiana Fairchild, who disappeared in December 1999. The child&#39;s remains were later found in a remote area of the Santa Cruz mountains. Anderson could receive the death penalty if convicted. <BR> <BR>After his arrest for the abduction of the eight-year-old girl, Anderson boasted that he was also responsible for kidnapping and killing Xiana Fairchild. He has since recanted those statements.&#34; <BR> <BR>But you know that recanting business is just crap. He murdered Xiana. Glad to know he&#39;s behind bars, anyway.  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12647-->
<!--Post: 12649--><!--Time: 1112678219-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12649"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:kathiemoffett@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Dahlia<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dahlia<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 10:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTW, I recently read &#34;Stalemate,&#34; the book mentioned by Weeza and others above. <BR> <BR>I CANNOT recommend it highly enough. It is an absolute must-read for anyone interested in these cases, or in child abductors/killers, period. Superbly written and researched, an exemplar of what a true crime book should be.  <BR> <BR>But what I like best about it is that, for the most part, it lets Bindner speak for himself; you, the reader, are allowed to develop your own opinion. Hence, you are in a sense put in the same awful position as LE and the missing girls&#39; parents-- Bindner really messes with your mind just as he&#39;s done with them for so long. <BR> <BR>One thing puzzled me. The author really underplayed the significance to Bindner of Lewis Carroll--he barely touched on it and never even mentioned that Carroll has long been considered by  modern scholars to have been, at the very least, highly inappropriate in his relations with little girls. Bindner quoted Carroll extensively and bears strong resemblances to the man in many ways, including appearance. I think this train of thought might be worth pursuing. <BR> <BR>Weeza, any word on what Bindner is doing these days? Does he still live in Oakland? The whole case has a particularly creepy aspect for me personally, as I lived near Bindner for many years, my parents are buried in one of the Oakland cemeteries he haunts, and, quite frankly, I recognize the guy.  <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12649-->
